From: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
Subject: [PATCH] net/sched: sch_cake: share the cake_mq rate by backlog and flow weight

cake_mq gives every TX queue that was active since the last sync an equal
part of the rate. A queue whose flows are limited further on, such as
traffic to a slow Wi-Fi station, keeps a part it cannot use, and the
link stays below the configured rate. The split also ignores flows and
hosts: two bulk flows hashed onto one queue get half the rate of a single
flow on another, and with host isolation a host whose flows land on
several queues counts as a separate host in each of them, unlike with one
CAKE instance.

Let each instance estimate the rate it sends at and count as backlogged
when it sends nearly all of its rate or queues more than 1 ms of it. A
sibling that is not backlogged keeps its estimate plus 1/16, and the
backlogged instances share the rest in proportion to their bulk flows,
each weighted by its quantum. A sibling counts as active for 4 ms after
it last sent, so a slow queue keeps its part between two packets.

With host isolation, each instance looks up the host of each bulk flow in
the host tables of the active siblings every 10 ms, by the tag that the
hash with the global seed gives in every instance, and adds the bulk
flows found there when it sizes the quantum. In the srchost, dsthost and
hosts modes, where each flow is a host, it looks up the flow tag instead.
The sync step reads a few fields of each sibling with READ_ONCE() and no
locks, and the stores to the tags, the host bulk counts and the flow set
use WRITE_ONCE().

On a veth with four TX queues and cake_mq at 100 Mbit/s, one flow on
queue 0 and one on queue 1 to a receiver behind a 20 Mbit/s tbf now
reach 93.3-93.4 instead of 87.6-89.4 Mbit/s of goodput in total. Two
flows on queue 0 and one on queue 1 get 31.7-32.1 Mbit/s each instead of
24 and 48, as with a single cake. With dual-dsthost, a host with four
flows spread over the queues and a host with one flow on queue 0 get
48.0-48.3 and 47.8 Mbit/s instead of 83.6-83.8 and 11.9-12.0. In all six
host isolation modes, with the four flows spread or with each host on a
queue of its own, the hosts get 47.7-49.4 and 47.7-48.2 Mbit/s, where a
single cake gives 47.9-48.9 and 47.3-47.9 (3 runs each, QEMU). With 64
hosts that each have a bulk flow on all four queues, a lookup round
takes 8-18 us per instance every 10 ms under QEMU emulation.

Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
---
The net-next patch moves this into cake_mq_share_rate(); here it stays in
cake_dequeue() so that hack-6.18/710 keeps its context.

